        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why the metropolitan region matters",
        "content": "Iran’s Habitat III national-report summary describes urban expansion beyond Tehran’s city limits into surrounding towns and settlements. It also identifies Mashhad, Tabriz, Isfahan, and Shiraz as important metropolitan regions. These are historical findings from the conference reporting period, rather than a verified description of conditions in 2026. [s3]\n\nThe distinction matters for interpreting the landscape. A municipal boundary marks an administrative jurisdiction; it does not necessarily enclose the wider urban system. The national report consequently argues for planning at a regional scale. Its account of approved plans establishes the government’s reported planning activity, rather than demonstrating that those plans achieved their intended outcomes. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "housing-services-environment",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Expansion and the quality of urban life",
        "content": "Research on Tehran’s land-use efficiency asks whether outward growth actually improves access to urban services. The inspected study summary reports that expansion did not translate into corresponding improvements in service availability and accessibility. This is a case-specific finding, not a measurement of every Iranian city. [s4]\n\nEnvironmental research adds another dimension. A Tehran vegetation study uses satellite imagery and spatial analysis to examine the relationship between construction, population density, and vegetation loss. Its findings associate construction-land expansion and increasing density with declining vegetation cover. Such evidence helps explain why a map of new buildings cannot, by itself, describe the quality of urban development. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "exploring-the-branches",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the urban landscape",
        "content": "The branches of this guide approach urbanization through complementary questions. Metropolitan growth concerns the scale and distribution of expansion. Informal settlements concern the relationship between affordable shelter, planning systems, and access to services. New towns, transport, housing, and neighborhood renewal offer further ways to investigate how urban change is organized.\n\nThese subjects should be read together. Iran’s national report emphasizes regional coordination, while research on Tehran questions whether physical expansion alone delivers better services. The useful conclusion is therefore an analytical one: assess where growth occurs, whom it serves, and what evidence demonstrates improvements in everyday conditions. [s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
